{"parentUuid":"e8df5931-57cd-42f2-b011-f4514843d6b3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a11eb425ca2c37694","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1KCn3yaymqLk7KYVTTaH9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Huub Library - Technical Information\n\nBased on the provided web page content, here's what is available:\n\n## What the Page Contains\n\nThe page offers a **high-level overview** of Huub as a constraint-based optimization library. It describes Huub as combining \"constraint programming (CP) with the learning and proof machinery of Boolean satisfiability (SAT) solving.\"\n\nKey attributes mentioned:\n- **Reliability**: Well-tested with preprocessing and Rust implementation\n- **Performance**: Strong propagation and modern solving techniques\n- **Extensibility**: Allows custom propagators, branchers, and SAT solver replacement\n\n## What Is NOT Included\n\nThe provided content does **not contain**:\n- Installation or build steps\n- Usage examples or code snippets\n- FlatZinc or MiniZinc support details\n- Information about supported constraints\n- Python bindings documentation\n- Library embedding guidance\n\n## Where to Find This Information\n\nThe page references three external resources:\n1. **Website**: https://huub.solutions\n2. **Crates.io**: https://crates.io/crates/huub (Rust package registry)\n3. **docs.rs**: https://docs.rs/huub/latest/huub/ (Official documentation)\n\nThese linked resources would contain the technical implementation details, installation instructions, and comprehensive API documentation you're seeking."}]},"uuid":"128432e7-4a20-4075-adc1-49849b6accdc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:21.548Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e8df5931-57cd-42f2-b011-f4514843d6b3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f565e20a-72ad-42bd-bda3-de60ed47276a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a11eb425ca2c37694","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SZEFZkPGGGrdDyx6ajxis3","type":"tool_result","content":"# Document Analysis: Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\n\n## Abstract\n\n\"Lazy Clause Generation (LCG) is an architecture for building Constraint Programming (CP) solvers using an underlying Boolean Satisfiability (SAT) engine.\" The authors demonstrate that using the IPASIR-UP interface enables constructing highly competitive LCG solvers by swapping underlying SAT engines while adhering to interface constraints.\n\n## Section Headings\n\n1. Introduction\n2. Background and Related Work\n3. LCG Architecture with a Modular SAT Engine\n4. Experimental Evaluation\n   - 4.1 Comparison with State-of-the-art Solvers\n   - 4.2 Inprocessing in Modern SAT Solvers\n   - 4.3 Revisit Explanation Mechanisms\n   - 4.4 Disabling High-Level Propagation\n5. Conclusion\n\n## Benchmark Tables\n\n**Table 1:** MiniZinc Challenge 2024 comparison (SAT-based and Fixed search modes) showing PAR2 scores, optimal instances solved, unsatisfiable proofs, satisfiable solutions, and unknown outcomes.\n\n**Table 2:** Ablation study of inprocessing techniques (subsumption, variable elimination, failed literal probing, globally blocked clause elimination).\n\n**Table 3:** Threshold comparisons (L values: 0, 3, 5, ∞) for clausal explanation mechanisms.\n\n**Table 4:** Adaptive propagation mechanisms with different threshold values (T=10⁻², 10⁻⁴, 10⁻⁶).\n\n## Propagators Mentioned\n\nThe paper identifies that Huub implements propagators for \"all FlatZinc primitive constraints\" but only supports **all_different** and **disjunctive** global constraints. Other constraints decompose using MiniZinc's decompositions.\n\nNotably absent from Huub: **diffn**, **cumulative**, and **global difference** constraints—critical for specific problem classes.\n\n## Routing/Packing Constraints\n\nThe document does not mention routing, packing, no_overlap, or reachability constraints. References to **diffn** (non-overlapping constraints) and **cumulative** (scheduling) appear only as examples where CP-SAT and Chuffed substantially outperform Huub.\n\n## Comparative Performance Summary\n\n**Huub Advantages:** Superior on cable-tree-wiring, compression, fox-geese-corn, harmony, and word-equations problems.\n\n**Huub Disadvantages:** \"CP-SAT and Chuffed outperform Huub substantially\" on accap, community-detection, network_50_cstr, tiny-cvrp, train-scheduling, and yumi-dynamic—primarily because these require efficient global constraint propagators absent in Huub."}]},"uuid":"b94df5e4-1a95-4206-89d4-ad6326686ac0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:26.170Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f565e20a-72ad-42bd-bda3-de60ed47276a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
